  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Above seasonal temperatures and rain-free \nconditions will prevail throughout the week. \n\nHigh pressure has begun to build over the central US today with\ndeep dry northwesterly flow building over the Carolinas. This \nupper high will slowly expand and drift eastward into the Mid- \nAtlantic through the end of the week. Dry conditions will \nprevail with subsidence over the area through at least Friday. \nTemperatures will be on the warmer side with the strong upper \nridge overhead. High temperatures will run well above normal in \nthe upper 80s and possibly a few spots hitting 90. We could \neasily flirt with daily record highs by the middle part of the \nweek.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Breezy with temperatures well-below normal this \nweekend.\n\nA strong cold front will push across southeast Georgia and the \nSC Lowcountry late Friday night into Saturday morning. Medium \nrange guidance generally agrees that it should be a dry cold \nfront, only sprinkles across portions of the forecast area late \nFriday night into early Saturday morning. GFS H85 temperatures \nover Lake Moultrie cool from 17C at 0Z Sat to 8C by 0Z Sun. As a\nresult, temperature will fall well below normal on Sat, with \nhighs ranging around 70 degrees. In addition, north-northeast \nwinds across the region will be breezy, with gusts between 25-30\nmph. \n\nSaturday night into Sunday, the center of a 1037 mb high will \nsettle over VA by daybreak Sunday. The sfc high will ridge SW \nacross the Carolinas and Georgia on Sunday. North winds will \ncontinue Saturday night into Sunday morning, generally between \n10-15 mph with gusts around 20 mph. The north winds will \nmaintain cool llvl thickness across the forecast area. Using a \nblend of guidance, low temperatures on Sunday are forecast to \nrange from the upper 40s inland to the mid to upper 50s along \nthe coast. Some areas are expected see their coolest \ntemperatures since early May.\n\nAs inland sfc ridging continues, gusty northeast winds should \nremain across the region on Sunday. Lingering cool llvl \nthicknesses along with some cloud cover across the region should\nlimit heating on Sunday. High temperatures are forecast to \nrange from the upper 60s across the Lowcountry to the low 70s \nacross SE GA. The center of sfc high pressure should shift over \noff the New England coast on Monday, resulting in winds to \nweaken and shift from the east. Temperatures across the CWA \nshould warm around 5 degrees over Sunday's readings.",
